WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
Let's start with a reality check about React Native's new architecture.

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.500
Writing standard TypeScript for React means flexibility.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.600
Types can be dynamic, loose, and forgiving.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.840
But when you build a turbo module, that flexibility disappears.

5
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.880
The TypeScript spec file serves as the absolute single source of truth for the native interface.

6
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.960
This file isn't being fed into a standard JavaScript bundler to be served to the client.

7
00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.800
Instead, it acts as a rigid schema that drives a specialized build-time code generator.

8
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.920
Feed a single TypeScript file into the CodeGen engine,

9
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.520
and it mathematically derives three parallel streams of code.

10
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.960
Java, Objective C++, and C++.

11
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.640
These generated interfaces are strictly and permanently bound to that initial TypeScript input.

12
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.800
Because CodeGen translates your TypeScript into static C++ headers,

13
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.320
standard JavaScript type maneuvering simply won't compile.

14
00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.440
The C++ layer requires strict memory boundaries.

15
00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:05.520
Writing a valid turbo module requires treating the TypeScript spec as a cross-language contract,

16
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.400
rather than a collection of optional type-ends.

17
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.240
This strictness begins before CodeGen even parses your types.

18
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.360
There are non-negotiable naming rules you must follow just to get the engine to look at your file.

19
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.200
First, your file name must start with the exact prefix native.

20
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.440
If you name it something like calculator.ts, CodeGen won't throw an error.

21
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.840
It will simply and silently ignore the file,

22
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.480
leaving you without your native interfaces.

23
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.640
Once inside the correctly named style,

24
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.760
the exported interface must be named exactly spec,

25
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.960
and this spec interface is required to explicitly extend the base turbo module type.

26
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.720
Try to use conventional interface naming like iSpec or MySpec,

27
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.680
and the parser immediately stops, throwing a misnamed module interface parser error.

28
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.760
Then there is the registration string.

29
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.640
Below the interface, you invoke the module registry.

30
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:58.000
The string you pass into the get enforcing function,

31
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.240
in this case, native calculator,

32
00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.600
must perfectly match the name used to register the native class.

33
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.920
Specifically, this exact string has to align perfectly with the return value of the get name method

34
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.520
over on the native side.

35
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.760
If there is a typo or a missbatch here, it won't be caught at compile time.

36
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.840
It results in a runtime invariant violation when the app tries to load the module.

37
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.040
Finally, the file must use a .ts or .ts x extension.

38
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.440
If you use .js, the parser assumes you are writing flow specifications.

39
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.720
These naming conventions operate as a strict entry gate.

40
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.600
Fail them, and the compilation pipeline aborts before the type translation even begins.

41
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.400
When you execute the build by running generate code gen artifacts from schema on Android,

42
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.920
or pod install on iOS, the real work begins.

43
00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.520
These commands activate a specialized type script parser built directly

44
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.000
into the React Native code gen library.

45
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.720
This diagram represents the validation phase.

46
00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.360
Think of the parser as a rigid filter.

47
00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.840
We are taking the dynamic types of typescript,

48
00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.000
and forcing them into the static structural constraints of C++.

49
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.760
This translation step means that using an unsupported dynamic type

50
00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.880
triggers a hard build error rather than a simple inter-warning.

51
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.640
Take the typescript utility type partial of T.

52
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.800
C++ structs require explicit immutable structural boundaries.

53
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.640
A dynamic partial object cannot be safely mapped to a static C++ structure at compile time.

54
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.360
To pass the parser, you must manually expand partial of T into a new type definition,

55
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.400
where every optional field is explicitly declared.

56
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.480
Another common failure point is declaring primitive types as nullable,

57
00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.800
like a union of a number and null.

58
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.640
A primitive number in C++ cannot hold a null state.

59
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.280
The memory allocated for an integer or a double expects a numerical value.

60
00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.680
You have two architectural work-arounds.

61
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.560
The first is to use a numerical Sentinel value,

62
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.120
like assigning negative one for the null state.

63
00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.480
The alternative is wrapping the primitive inside an object,

64
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.880
defining a value property mapped to a number,

65
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.440
and making the entire container nullable.

66
00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.000
Every type you define in the spec must possess a mathematically perfect translation

67
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.760
to a static native type.

68
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.560
If it doesn't map exactly, the boundary integrity breaks.

69
00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.200
Once your spec passes validation,

70
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.880
the Code Gen Engine proceeds to generate the required interface files.

71
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.720
These artifacts are written directly to the build directory inside your project.

72
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.640
For Android, Code Gen outputs an abstract Java class

73
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.880
that your module will need to extend,

74
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.120
alongside the underlying C++ JSI glue headers.

75
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.160
For iOS, it generates an objective C++ protocol your code must adopt,

76
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.640
along with its corresponding JSI bindings.

77
00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.880
There is one unbreakable rule regarding these artifacts,

78
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.320
because they are derived from the TypeScript spec on every build,

79
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:50.720
they must never be edited manually.

80
00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:54.160
This rigid output structure introduces an architectural challenge

81
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.400
when dealing with large modules that contain dozens of complex methods.

82
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.440
Defining massive, all-encompassing spec files results in monolithic generated classes

83
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.880
that are difficult to maintain and implement on the native side.

84
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.440
The elegant solution is spec composition,

85
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.560
splitting large modules into multiple distinct specs based on their specific domains.

86
00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.000
This workflow shows how composition operates at build time.

87
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.080
Each domain's specific TypeScript spec you write generates its own independent

88
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.240
locked set of native interfaces.

89
00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.920
On the native registry side,

90
00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.880
each of these generated specs maps to its own distinct registered module class.

91
00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.240
However, you aren't forced to duplicate your actual logic.

92
00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.760
These split native classes can still call through to a single

93
00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.720
shared internal implementation behind the scenes.

94
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.840
For example, you can route two different storage specs

95
00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.200
to the same underlying shared preferences wrapper on Android

96
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.440
or NS user data on iOS,

97
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.160
keeping your code modular with redundancy.

98
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.560
Because the build directory operates as an automated untouchable translation layer,

99
00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.760
adopting modular spec design is essential for keeping your resulting native code base organized.

100
00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.720
From the rigid naming rules applied to your TypeScript input,

101
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.600
down to the uneditable locked native outputs,

102
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:10.560
the code gen pipeline operates as a strict governor,

103
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:13.840
enforcing these validation rules at the very beginning of the process

104
00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.000
guarantees mathematical type safety across the native C++ boundary.

105
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.680
By respecting this contract,

106
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:23.600
you eliminate the vast majority of cross-language communication bugs

107
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.160
before the application even attempts to compile.

108
00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.920
Now that this spec has generated these locked artifacts,

109
00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.720
the next phase is crossing over into the native layer

110
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.160
and implementing those typed interfaces in iOS and Android.

111
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.680
Mastering the strict language of the TypeScript spec

112
00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.200
provides the structural foundation required to engineer successfully

113
00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.600
within React Native's new architecture.

